WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.730
A useful OT alert describes a condition that matters and provides enough context for

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:05.720
action.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.600
Examples include an unauthorized remote session, new communication across a boundary,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.545
a programming event outside maintenance, an unexpected device,

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:15.265
repeated authentication failure, monitoring loss,

00:00:15.265 --> 00:00:17.700
or a change to a protected configuration.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.145
Triage should identify the source and destination assets, protocol, time, user or session,

00:00:22.145 --> 00:00:26.100
asset function, criticality, process state, related change records,

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.542
and supporting telemetry.

00:00:27.542 --> 00:00:30.880
Analysts should determine whether the observation is approved, unexplained,

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.570
a process fault, a security-control failure, or evidence of a cyber event.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:37.735
Operations and engineering teams are investigation partners.

00:00:37.735 --> 00:00:42.400
They can confirm maintenance, explain equipment behavior, identify process consequences,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.918
and advise which containment actions are safe.

00:00:44.918 --> 00:00:48.985
A security analyst may recognize unusual network behavior while an operator recognizes

00:00:48.985 --> 00:00:51.820
that it coincides with a valve fault or production transition.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.104
Preserve evidence before it is overwritten.

00:00:54.104 --> 00:00:57.985
Record alert details, packet or log references, affected assets, timelines,

00:00:57.985 --> 00:01:00.590
analyst decisions, and communications.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:04.815
Protect evidence integrity and control access because OT telemetry may reveal sensitive

00:01:04.815 --> 00:01:06.560
architecture and operations.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.790
Escalation workflows should define severity, contacts, response authority,

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.355
and expected time.

00:01:11.355 --> 00:01:14.000
High-consequence conditions may require immediate coordination,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.440
but automated isolation should be used cautiously.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:21.120
Disconnecting a system can remove operator visibility, redundancy, or required control.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.958
Close the loop after investigation.

00:01:22.958 --> 00:01:26.470
Document the disposition, update inventory or baselines, tune detection,

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.960
correct the underlying control, and capture lessons for response plans.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:34.395
Alert quality improves when false positives and missed context are treated as engineering

00:01:34.395 --> 00:01:36.270
feedback rather than merely analyst workload.
